5. EDGE-CASE TESTING
Empty states: Replaced by fabricated demo data — new users see a fake "Science · Tesla (sample)" calendar and a hardcoded "Polynomials" lesson on Home ((tabs)/index.tsx:284-290, schedule.ts:236). This is misleading; some demo entries reference plan IDs that don't exist, producing dead taps (agenda.ts:651-685).
Invalid input: Lesson-plan form validates well (6-month cap, date ranges, exam uniqueness). Subject/lesson forms accept free-text numbers and only catch collisions at save. AI input has no length cap (cost risk).
Duplicate records: Username/email duplicates surface as raw constraint errors on edit (no handling in settings.tsx).
Large datasets: Dashboard loads all slots+blocks for all plans every focus; library signs every image individually (N+1); subject tree is unvirtualized. Fine for a few plans, slow for a heavy user.
Slow/failed network: useSubscription falls back to free-tier limits, briefly showing paid users a spurious paywall (useSubscription.ts:37-60).
Session expiration / cold start: supabase.ts:18 sets persistSession:true without an explicit AsyncStorage adapter — verify sessions survive cold start, or users get logged out every launch.
Browser refresh / re-entry: Onboarding/admin route gating is racy (_layout.tsx vs index.tsx both redirect) — brief exposure of (tabs) before the async role/onboarding check resolves.
6. PRODUCTION READINESS — ALPHA
Reasoning: a security hole that hands any user superadmin; three of four login paths broken; payments non-functional in the launch market; a launch-blocking DB constraint bug on a core action; unusable dark mode; and multiple dead/stub features. The billing schema and scheduler are Beta-quality in isolation, but the app as a whole is Alpha.

7. FEATURE-GAP ANALYSIS (vs. what a teacher reasonably expects)
Must-have (missing): working username/OAuth login & password reset; functional payment in PH; export/print a lesson plan (todo:117); a real empty state instead of fake data; password-strength validation; email-confirmation gating; a way for admins to sign out.
Should-have: attendance, gradebook/scoring, student roster, announcements (if you intend to be more than a planner); admin write/CRUD (acknowledged read-only in todo:114); notification preferences (todo:113).
Nice-to-have: parent access, analytics dashboards, calendar export (.ics), collaborative/department plan sharing.

9. DATABASE AUDIT
Overall: The schema is, structurally, the strongest part of the project — consistent public_id patterns, thorough indexing, sensible FK on delete rules, CHECK constraints (e.g. blocks_session_pair_check), updated_at triggers, tier-enforcing security definer RPCs, and webhook idempotency tables. But it has several serious correctness/security defects.

Database Findings
DB-1 — Privilege escalation via unrestricted users self-update — CRITICAL

Affected: public.users, all RLS that trusts role.
The policy "users can update own profile" (00_users.sql:84-88) is USING (auth.uid()=userID) WITH CHECK (auth.uid()=userID) with no column restriction, and role lives on this table. There is no trigger guarding the column (grep confirms role is only read by helpers, never protected). Any authenticated user can run supabase.from('users').update({ role:'superadmin' }).eq('userid', myId) and become a superadmin, satisfying is_current_user_admin()/is_current_user_school_admin() and unlocking admin reads of every school member's profile and all plans.
Fix before launch: add a BEFORE UPDATE trigger that rejects NEW.role <> OLD.role unless the caller is already admin (or move role to a table users can't write, and REVOKE UPDATE(role)). Risk: Critical.
DB-2 — units has RLS disabled — CRITICAL (tenant isolation)

Affected: public.units. 06_rls.sql:7-22 enables RLS on every academic table except units, and no policy is ever created for it. With Supabase's default grants, any authenticated user can read and write all rows in units across all tenants. Add units to the RLS list with a subject-membership policy mirroring chapters. Risk: High–Critical.
DB-3 — courses / user_courses tables are never created — CRITICAL (schema not reproducible)

Affected: subjects.course_id FK (04_academics.sql:9), 06_rls.sql:11-12,152-195, indexes 04_academics.sql:37-39. These objects are referenced by FK, RLS, and indexes but there is no CREATE TABLE public.courses/user_courses anywhere in database-setup/. Running the migrations fresh (as todo:19 instructs for the prod project) will fail at the subjects FK or at the RLS ALTER. The current dev DB only works because these tables exist from history not captured in the repo. Risk: Critical for the prod cutover.
DB-4 — user_schools has no UPDATE or DELETE policy — HIGH

Affected: user_schools. Only SELECT/INSERT policies exist (06_rls.sql:94-105). So the app's "set primary institution" (UPDATE) and "delete institution" (DELETE membership) silently affect 0 rows under RLS — the UI reports success while nothing changes. Also blocks any future "admin assigns teacher to department" (no UPDATE on department_id). Risk: High (data/UX integrity).
DB-5 — subscriptions has no admin read policy — HIGH (feature broken)

Affected: subscriptions (08_billing.sql:97-100, read is auth.uid()=user_id only). The admin "X of Y teachers on Pro" count and per-teacher tier badges join subscriptions and get null for everyone but the viewer → counts ~always 0, all badges "FREE." Risk: High (misleading admin data).
DB-6 — Any school member can manage school-wide calendar events — HIGH

Affected: school_calendar_events (06_rls.sql:464-482, FOR ALL to any member). One teacher can insert/delete holidays/suspensions affecting all teachers in the school. Combined with join_school_by_code (any authenticated user can join any school given an 8-char code, no rate limit — 10_departments.sql:99), this is a cross-user disruption vector. Scope event management to the creator or to admins. Risk: High.
DB-7 — Webhook fail-open: paid → tier free — HIGH

Affected: subscriptions. Both webhook handlers default an unmatched plan_id/price_id to tier='free', status='active' (paymongo-webhook:127-129, stripe-webhook:136-138), and the env plan IDs default to "". A misconfiguration silently leaves paying users on free. Fail closed (log + 5xx). Risk: High (revenue).
DB-8 — Seed/placeholder create real accounts with hardcoded passwords — HIGH/CRITICAL if run in prod

Affected: auth.users. placeholder.sql:38 inserts six confirmed users (one admin) with password ScheduDemo2026!; 09_test_account_seed.sql:48 inserts a tier2 test user with ScheduTest2026!. If either is ever applied to production, these are known-credential backdoors (one admin). Gate them behind an explicit dev-only guard and never include in the prod run. Risk: Critical if mis-run.
DB-9 — Migration ordering hazard — MEDIUM

09_test_account_seed.sql:123 writes usage_quotas.period_month, which 10_billing_v2.sql:243 drops. Applying 10 before 09 breaks the seed. The file headers disagree on order; document and enforce 00→11.
DB-10 — is_default modeling + non-atomic multi-writes — MEDIUM

schools.is_default is a school-level flag the client toggles per-user "set primary," conflating two concepts; and several flows do 2–4 sequential writes with no transaction (set-primary, delete cascade, plan creation). Move each to a single security definer RPC. Risk: Medium.
DB-11 — No audit/history on destructive edits except plan rebalance — MEDIUM

Lesson/exam/WW/PT saves are destructive overwrites; only lesson_plan_versions exists (and "restore" is lossy — doesn't re-add deleted or remove added blocks, rebalance-service.ts:786-808). No general audit log.
Performance/scalability for 1k / 10k / 100k:

1k: fine. 10k: mostly fine; watch the unscoped dashboard fetch and N+1 image signing (client-side, not DB). 100k: the per-isolate in-memory rate limiter (_shared/rate-limit.ts) and x-forwarded-for-based webhook limiting must move to a shared store; RLS helper subqueries (is_current_user_school_admin) are stable so cached per-statement — acceptable. Indexing is good. No materialized aggregates needed yet.
Database Health Score: 55 / 100. Database Production Readiness: Alpha.

DB changes required before launch: (1) guard users.role; (2) enable RLS + policy on units; (3) add CREATE TABLE courses/user_courses (or remove the references) so migrations are reproducible; (4) add UPDATE/DELETE policies on user_schools; (5) admin read policy on subscriptions; (6) scope school_calendar_events writes; (7) make webhooks fail-closed on unknown plan/price; (8) quarantine seed/placeholder from prod; (9) verify migration order; (10) move RPC-worthy multi-writes server-side.

