11. SECURITY AUDIT (each: Severity / Impact / Likelihood / Recommendation)
Role self-escalation (DB-1) — Critical / full admin takeover / High (trivial via the public anon key + RLS) / guard the column. This is the headline finding.
units RLS disabled (DB-2) — High / cross-tenant read/write of curriculum / High / enable RLS.
Schema not reproducible — courses missing (DB-3) — High (operational) / prod migration fails / High on cutover / add the tables.
Username login leak risk — Medium / if anyone "fixes" username login by opening anon SELECT on users, it leaks emails by username / Medium / resolve via a security definer RPC returning an opaque token, not the email.
Webhook fail-open + no timestamp/replay window (DB-7) — High/Medium / revenue loss; stale-but-valid replays accepted (idempotency only stops same-id) / Medium / fail-closed + reject |now−t|>300s.
Rate limiting ineffective — Medium / per-isolate in-memory + spoofable x-forwarded-for; extract-text and delete-account have no limit / Medium / shared store; rely on signature+idempotency, not IP.
Client-side-only admin gating — Medium / any user can render (admin) by deep link (RLS still protects data — except DB-1 nullifies that) / Medium / server-verified role gate.
Prompt injection / unbounded AI input — Medium (abuse, not breach) / user text overrides system prompt and can balloon token cost on your key / Medium / cap input length; keep system prompt authoritative.
Info disclosure — Low–Medium / edge functions echo upstream error details and extractorUrl to clients / Medium / return generic errors, log server-side.
Weak password policy — Low–Medium / 8-char min only, no strength/breach check (todo:116 open) / High to occur / add zxcvbn-style check.
No email-confirmation gate on plan creation (todo:110 open) — Low.
Positive confirmations (verified): webhooks verify HMAC (constant-time) and are idempotent; delete-account deletes only the authenticated id (no IDOR); quota RPCs run as the user, not service role; subscriptions/usage_quotas/billing_events have client INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE revoked — no client path can self-grant a tier (the only tier-write path is the signed webhook). Aside from DB-1, the billing security model is sound.

12. ROLE-BASED ACCESS TESTING
Teacher: correct data scoping via RLS — until DB-1 lets them self-promote. The 8-char join_school_by_code (no rate limit) lets any teacher join any school whose code they obtain/guess.
Admin / Superadmin: read access to school members/plans works; cannot see teacher subscription tiers (DB-5); admin screens are read-only; admins have no in-app sign-out.
Student / Parent: no such roles exist (user_role enum = teacher/admin/superadmin). Not applicable.
URL/deep-link bypass: (admin) is reachable by any authenticated user (client-gate only); data is RLS-protected (modulo DB-1/DB-2).
13. RESPONSIVE DESIGN (inferred from layout code; verify on device)
320–414px (mobile): primary target. Risks: calendar month bars use 9px text in 38px rows (truncation), block-editor step cards at 47% width leave an awkward center gap, the very long lesson-plan form is dense. Toolbar month label (26px) can clip on 320px.
768–820px (tablet): no tablet-specific layouts; everything is single-column phone UI stretched — usable but not optimized.
1366–1920px (desktop): this is an Expo/React-Native Android app; teach-web is a near-empty Next.js shell (one page.tsx). Desktop is effectively out of scope.
Worst offenders for overflow/unusability: dark-mode screens (white text on white), Android inline DateTimePicker (renders as a dialog, breaking the two-column "Starts/Ends" layout).
14. ACCESSIBILITY — score 28 / 100
No accessibilityLabel/accessibilityRole on icon-only controls; no accessibilityLiveRegion on the toast; inputs have placeholders but no associated labels or focus states; ErrorBoundary uses #666 on #111 (fails WCAG AA); WebView content is outside the a11y tree; no keyboard submit. This needs a dedicated pass.

15. ERROR HANDLING
The AI/edge-function paths are the best in the app (402→paywall, structured formatEdgeFunctionError, empty-text guards). Everywhere else is weak: loaders swallow errors into empty states; settings.tsx can corrupt the profile on a failed-load→save; handleSaveDetails is try/finally with no catch; non-atomic multi-writes leave partial state with no rollback; ErrorBoundary only console.errors (no Sentry, despite initSentry() at root); "Try Again" just re-renders the failing tree (loop).

16. DATA CONSISTENCY
Admin term card: differenceInDays argument order inverted → "0 days left" and wrong progress % ((admin)/index.tsx:264-277).
Plan status computed differently on dashboard vs plans list → same plan shows two statuses.
Requirement counts count every block (incl. quizzes and split fragments) → stepper numbers ≠ real requirement counts; the "−" deletion comparator is non-transitive and can delete a placed block.
Term-progress "Week N": fencepost (term-start-anchored 7-day windows, not Mon-anchored) and counts calendar days incl. weekends.
No grade/score calculations exist to be wrong — grading "components" are AI text only.
List counts can understate reality (rows dropped when a join is missing, e.g. subject_code).
17. PERFORMANCE — bottlenecks
Client-side: unscoped dashboard fetch (all slots+blocks, every focus); N+1 signed-URL generation for subject images; full subject_detail reload on focus + unvirtualized tree; every block edit triggers two full loadRuntimeData round-trips (~7 queries each) + per-row UPDATE loops; WebView remounts keyed on html.length. Server-side: per-isolate rate limiting. None are fatal at soft-launch scale; all matter at 10k+.

18. CODE QUALITY — maintainability 48 / 100
Good: clean Expo Router structure, a genuinely well-decomposed scheduling algorithm with tests, consistent SQL conventions. Bad: ~90% duplicated code across the four detail screens (already caused the ww_detail missing-filter bug); two parallel scheduling engines; empty shared-types package → everything untyped (as any), which is why the null subscription joins and broken add-requirement insert weren't caught at compile time; @ts-nocheck on every edge function; dead files (plan_history.tsx 0 bytes, pdf-extractor.tsx, likely-unused BottomSheet.tsx); four duplicate formatTime12 and two duplicate todayISO helpers; pervasive local-vs-UTC date handling.

19. DEPLOYMENT READINESS
Per the devs' own todo, most ops prerequisites are open: prod Supabase project not provisioned; scheduhq.com not purchased; PayMongo/Stripe accounts, plan/price IDs, and webhook secrets not set; edge functions not deployed; Sentry not installed (so the ErrorBoundary captures nothing); expo-localization not installed (so region detection fails → PH users routed to Stripe in USD); EAS build not configured; no privacy policy/ToS. .env is untracked (good). No backup/recovery/monitoring strategy documented. The Qwen→Gemini provider drift (function deployed uses GEMINI_API_KEY while todo:8 says rotate Qwen) means the documented secret may not match what's deployed.

20–21. BUSINESS LOGIC & COMPETITOR GAP
As a planner, ScheduTeach has a real differentiator: a DepEd-calendar-aware scheduler that auto-distributes lessons/quizzes/PTs/exams and rebalances around suspensions, plus AI activity generation tied to the curriculum. Nothing in Google Classroom / Canvas / Moodle / Schoology does the automatic schedule generation piece as a first-class feature — that's your moat.

But against those LMSs it lacks everything classroom-facing: no roster, gradebook, submissions, attendance, announcements, parent portal, or analytics. That's fine if you position it strictly as a teacher's personal planning companion. If you market it as a classroom platform, the gap is enormous. Decide the positioning before launch and make the marketing match.

